  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Determine the value of the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}8{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Also, note that $N=6,$ since there are $6$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=8{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =8{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0-1} \\\\  & =8{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=24,r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =24\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}]}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =36\\cdot (\\frac{728}{729}) \\\\  & \u224835.951\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places.",
